<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vijay Stambha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th century</span> · Also known as Tower of Victory</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vijay Stambha, or Tower of Victory, is a nine-storey tower in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittor-fort">Chittor Fort</a> built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a> of Mewar in the mid-fifteenth century to commemorate his victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-khalji">Mahmud Khalji</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. Rising about 37 metres and dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, it is covered inside and out with sculptures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> deities and has become an emblem of Chittor and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vijay-stambha.jpg" alt="Vijay Stambha" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Schwiki · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tower_of_victory.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
